    "result_text": "The full build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required validating the E.164 telephone's national-number length against its country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting with 400 when wrong.\n\nThe validation flows through `@TelephoneValidation` on the owner DTO \u2192 `TelephoneValidator` \u2192 `E164Telephone.isValid`/`normalize`. I made the change in the one place that matters:\n\n**`E164Telephone.java`** \u2014 after the existing 8\u201315 total-digit check, I added a per-country national-length rule driven by a small table:\n- `\"61\"` \u2192 national number must be exactly 9 digits\n- `\"1\"` \u2192 national number must be exactly 10 digits\n\nThe country code is matched against the leading digits of the E.164 form (ordered longest-prefix-first, unambiguous since `61` and `1` don't overlap). If a known country code matches, the national length (`digits.length() - countryCode.length()`) must equal the required value or `normalize` returns `null` (\u2192 invalid \u2192 400). Unknown country codes keep the existing general 8\u201315 behavior, so nothing else regresses. The normalization stays idempotent, and the default-AU path (`0412345678` \u2192 drop `0` \u2192 `+61412345678`, 9 national digits) still validates.\n\nI also updated the `@TelephoneValidation` default message to describe the new rule.\n\nTests: `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123`) passes, and the full suite is **BUILD SUCCESS** (1 test, 0 failures).\n\nNote: this is not a git repository, so there's nothing to commit.",
